MULTISYNTH TODO

1. CANONICAL CONTROL STATE IMPLEMENTATION
   Core rule: the module remembers; the control borrows.
   - On rebind, module state -> control: the newly bound parameter/state object completely repaints the control.
   - On user interaction, control -> module state: permitted changes are written back to the currently bound parameter/state object.
   - Physical controls may own only transient interaction state such as pointer ID, drag origin, active/pressed/turning feedback, gesture-local history, pointer capture, and interaction-only animation/timer state.
   - Freewheel/Test Module remains the intentional exception: when no real module/carrier is bound, the control may maintain temporary dummy state so its gesture and physical behavior can be tested.

   CONTROLS TO IMPLEMENT
   - KNOB
     Persistent bound state: { value, locked }.
     Rebind must repaint value and lock state from the bound parameter object.
     Turning writes value back only when the bound parameter is unlocked.
     Lock must not be authoritative on the physical knob instance.

   - ENCODER
     Persistent bound state: { value } plus any real per-binding semantic state already supported by the module.
     Rebind must repaint the represented value/rotation from the bound state.
     Turning writes the new value back through the current binding.

   - FADER
     Persistent bound state: { value }.
     Add/complete the external reflection path so rebind moves the physical thumb to the bound value.
     Dragging writes the new value back through the current binding.

   - RIBBON
     Persistent bound state: { value } where the module actually stores a lasting ribbon value.
     Add/complete the external reflection path so rebind moves the marker to the bound value.
     Touch/drag writes the new value back through the current binding.
     Return-to-zero/gesture animation remains transient control behavior where applicable.

   - SWITCH
     Persistent bound state: { on }.
     Add/complete the external reflection path so rebind immediately shows the bound on/off state.
     User toggle writes the new on/off state back through the current binding.

   - BUTTON / PAD
     Momentary press state remains transient physical state.
     If a module uses the control as latched/selected persistent state, that state belongs to the module binding and must repaint on rebind.
     User interaction writes any persistent latched/selected change back through the current binding.

   - XY
     Persistent bound state: { x, y }.
     Add/complete the external reflection path so rebind moves the dot to the bound X/Y coordinates.
     Dragging writes new X/Y values back through the current binding.

   - LED
     Persistent/semantic status is module-owned and normally flows one way: module state -> LED display.
     Add/complete an external state-reflection path; the LED must not become authority for the module state it indicates.

   - METER
     Displayed value is module/signal-owned and flows one way: source -> meter.
     Preserve the existing external repaint path; meter owns presentation only.

   - TURNTABLE
     Persistent bound state such as position and any existing module-owned playback/scrub state must come from the current binding.
     Rebind must fully repaint the represented persistent state.
     Scrub/user changes write permitted persistent changes back through the current binding.
     Pointer, active drag, last gesture angle/time, and similar gesture bookkeeping remain transient control state.

   CONTROLS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS STATE-OWNERSHIP IMPLEMENTATION
   - JACK: connection/routing state remains owned by the patch/routing system; the jack owns only patch gesture interaction.
   - DECAL: presentation only; no binding state.
   - OSCILLOSCOPE: streaming/source-driven display; no persistent controller-owned parameter state.
   - READOUT / SCREEN: display surfaces fed by external content/state; retain their existing explicit display/content paths rather than treating them as parameter controls.

   COMPLETION CONDITION
   - Every included control follows the same bounded contract: rebind reads complete persistent state from the new module binding; user interaction writes permitted changes to that binding; no persistent state from the previous binding leaks through the physical control.
   - Do not expand this item into unrelated controller redesign, leaf/module refactoring, visual changes, or broader architecture work.

2. CONTEXTUAL BINDING AUDIT — ALL MODULES
   - Audit every module for any physical control whose semantic target can change while the control remains mounted.
   - Give every contextual target a stable binding identity/key.
   - Binding selects which saved module state the physical control currently represents; the control must not infer context by inspecting the app.
   - Record each contextual use as: module | control | binding key | persistent fields | transient fields | rebind source | redraw complete? | leak risk.
   - Find and remove architectural dependence on hidden per-control persistent context state only after the audit and an explicitly agreed implementation plan.

3. REBIND / STATE-REFLECTION CONTRACT
   - On context change, the same physical control must bind to the newly selected saved-state target and completely reflect that target.
   - Rebinding must refresh every persistent property represented by the control, including value and per-binding lock/unlock state where applicable.
   - Example: Time Bandits probability must preserve independent probability + lock state for each drum; switching drums must show that drum's saved value and lock state immediately.
   - Interaction writes through the current binding into module state; changing context reads the new binding from module state back into the control.
   - No old binding's temporary interaction state may bleed into the new binding.

4. PERSISTENCE / RELOAD AUDIT
   - Verify contextual binding-associated persistent state survives save/reload for every affected module.
   - Reload must reconstruct the correct value, lock/unlock state, and other persistent contextual properties for each binding.
   - Verify switching among contexts after reload produces the same control state as before reload.

5. CONTROLLER OWNERSHIP PASS
   - Each unique controller type owns its appearance.
   - Each unique controller type owns its interaction behavior.
   - Each unique controller type owns its I/O contract.
   - Identical variants remain grouped under the same controller type.
   - Module-specific code must not reimplement controller appearance or interaction.

6. MODULE MAPPING PASS
   - After controller ownership is complete, review every module.
   - Map each module leaf implementation onto the canonical shared controllers.
   - The only unique module code should be the leaf implementation: what each control connects to and what its value means.

CANONICAL CONTROL LOCK — ACTIVE
   - The shared control library, including READOUT, is pure canon and untouchable by default.
   - Canon covers shared control appearance, geometry, interaction, generic I/O behavior, renderer behavior, and readout behavior/styling.
   - Module/leaf code may consume canonical controls and use their supported theme/CSS hooks without modifying canon.
   - Any change to an existing canonical control requires two explicit approvals for that exact change:
     1) Nate explicitly authorizes the specific proposed canonical change.
     2) The exact authorized scope is repeated back and Nate explicitly confirms it again.
   - Only after both approvals may that exact canonical change be executed.
   - Authorization is single-use and applies only to the confirmed change. It does not imply permission for related cleanup, refactors, adjacent controls, styling changes, behavior changes, or follow-up work.

LEAF CODE STATUS — SETTLED FOR NOW
   - Existing leaf implementations that currently work are considered settled for this pass.
   - Do not redesign, refactor, improve, or otherwise alter working leaf behavior while mapping modules to the shared controllers.
   - This is not a declaration that leaf implementations are permanently frozen or final.
   - Individual leaf behavior may be revisited later as the instruments evolve.
   - For now, preserve working leaf behavior while moving controller appearance, interaction, and generic I/O ownership into the canonical shared controllers.
